WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.260 --> 00:00:04.479
Dear Fellow Scholars, this is Two Minute Papers
with Károly Zsolnai-Fehér.

00:00:04.479 --> 00:00:09.430
Pose estimation is an interesting area of
research where we typically have a few images

00:00:09.430 --> 00:00:14.990
or video footage of humans, and we try to
automatically extract the pose a person was

00:00:14.990 --> 00:00:15.990
taking.

00:00:15.990 --> 00:00:21.010
In short, the input is one or more photo,
and the output is typically a skeleton of

00:00:21.010 --> 00:00:22.280
the person.

00:00:22.280 --> 00:00:24.050
So what is this good for?

00:00:24.050 --> 00:00:25.730
A lot of things.

00:00:25.730 --> 00:00:30.880
For instance, we can use these skeletons to
cheaply transfer the gestures of a human onto

00:00:30.880 --> 00:00:36.969
a virtual character, fall detection for the
elderly, analyzing the motion of athletes,

00:00:36.969 --> 00:00:39.120
and many many others.

00:00:39.120 --> 00:00:44.600
This work showcases a neural network that
measures how the wifi radio signals bounce

00:00:44.600 --> 00:00:49.879
around in the room and reflect off of the
human body, and from these murky waves, it

00:00:49.879 --> 00:00:51.829
estimates where we are.

00:00:51.829 --> 00:00:56.319
Not only that, but it also accurate enough
to tell us our pose.

00:00:56.319 --> 00:01:01.799
As you see here, as the wifi signal also traverses
in the dark, this pose estimation works really

00:01:01.799 --> 00:01:04.320
well in poor lighting conditions.

00:01:04.320 --> 00:01:06.050
That is a remarkable feat.

00:01:06.050 --> 00:01:10.920
But now, hold on to your papers, because that's
nothing compared to what you are about to

00:01:10.920 --> 00:01:12.160
see now.

00:01:12.160 --> 00:01:13.190
Have a look here.

00:01:13.190 --> 00:01:16.210
We know that wifi signals go through walls.

00:01:16.210 --> 00:01:21.560
So perhaps, this means that...that can't be
true, right?

00:01:21.560 --> 00:01:27.790
It tracks the pose of this human as he enters
the room, and now, as he disappears, look,

00:01:27.790 --> 00:01:30.480
the algorithm still knows where he is.

00:01:30.480 --> 00:01:31.480
That's right!

00:01:31.480 --> 00:01:35.390
This means that it can also detect our pose
through walls!

00:01:35.390 --> 00:01:37.440
What kind of wizardry is that?

00:01:37.440 --> 00:01:41.760
Now, note that this technique doesn't look
at the video feed we are now looking at.

00:01:41.760 --> 00:01:43.900
It is there for us for visual reference.

00:01:43.900 --> 00:01:49.610
It is also quite remarkable that the signal
being sent out is a thousand times weaker

00:01:49.610 --> 00:01:54.220
than an actual wifi signal, and it also can
detect multiple humans.

00:01:54.220 --> 00:01:58.990
This is not much of a problem with color images,
because we can clearly see everyone in an

00:01:58.990 --> 00:02:03.930
image, but the radio signals are more difficult
to read when they reflect off of multiple

00:02:03.930 --> 00:02:05.490
bodies in the scene.

00:02:05.490 --> 00:02:09.769
The whole technique work through using a teacher-student
network structure.

00:02:09.769 --> 00:02:14.519
The teacher is a standard pose estimation
neural network that looks at a color image

00:02:14.519 --> 00:02:17.390
and predicts the pose of the humans therein.

00:02:17.390 --> 00:02:19.959
So far, so good, nothing new here.

00:02:19.959 --> 00:02:24.970
However, there is a student network that looks
at the correct decisions of the teacher, but

00:02:24.970 --> 00:02:28.249
has the radio signal as an input instead.

00:02:28.249 --> 00:02:33.590
As a result, it will learn what the different
radio signal distributions mean and how they

00:02:33.590 --> 00:02:36.290
relate to human positions and poses.

00:02:36.290 --> 00:02:41.390
As the name says, the teacher shows the student
neural network the correct results, and the

00:02:41.390 --> 00:02:46.310
student learns how to produce them from radio
signals instead of images.

00:02:46.310 --> 00:02:50.829
If anyone said that they were working on this
problem ten years ago, they would have likely

00:02:50.829 --> 00:02:52.519
ended up in an asylum.

00:02:52.519 --> 00:02:54.889
Today, it's reality.

00:02:54.889 --> 00:02:56.370
What a time to be alive!

